// The accepting side of the traits whose description is a BUILT table rather
// than parsed source, and whose rows are the only description of them there is:
// Eq, its supertrait relationship with Hash, and Clone.
//
// A PARITY WITNESS rather than a floor: a wrong row in that table also changes
// what the rejection fixtures say, so they go red too. What only this file shows
// is that a correct program is still accepted - and that `Hash` finds its
// required `impl Eq` written AFTER it, which the supertrait question has to
// tolerate and no rejection exercises.
//
// `Display` is absent, and its absence is a measurement rather than an
// oversight: naming a `StringBuilder` anywhere makes the two emissions differ by
// two lines, so a Display impl cannot sit in a byte-parity fixture until that is
// settled. The refusing direction for Display is pinned in the rejection corpus,
// which compares messages and not artefacts.
struct P { x: int }

impl Hash for P {
    fn hash(*const self) -> u32 { return self.x as u32; }
}

impl Eq for P {
    fn eq(*const self, other: *const P) -> bool { return self.x == other.x; }
}

impl Clone for P {
    fn clone(*const self) -> P { return P { x: self.x }; }
}

fn main() {
    let p = P { x: 5 };
    let q = p.clone();
    println(p.eq(&q));
    println(p.hash() as int);
    println(q.x);
}
